    aa_nut_sprint: "<p><strong>CHO:</strong> 30–45 g total · Relies almost 100% on stored glycogen</p><p><strong>Fluids:</strong> 400–600 ml total · No time to hydrate during the swim</p><p><strong>Sodium:</strong> Minimal (~200 mg). Not enough time for critical losses</p><p><strong>Key strategy:</strong> 1 gel on the bike is enough. Don't overfuel: extra weight = slower</p>",
    aa_nut_olympic: "<p><strong>CHO:</strong> 60–90 g total · 1:1 glucose:fructose</p><p><strong>Fluids:</strong> 800–1200 ml total · ~500ml/hr</p><p><strong>Sodium:</strong> 400–600 mg total · An isotonic drink is enough</p><p><strong>Key strategy:</strong> Gel in T1 + gel/bar on the bike + gel on the run km 3–4. Total: 3–4 gels</p>",
    aa_nut_703: "<p><strong>CHO:</strong> 250–360 g total · 2:1 glucose:fructose required</p><p><strong>Fluids:</strong> 2.5–3.5 L total · 700ml/hr on the bike</p><p><strong>Sodium:</strong> 3000–5000 mg · Salt every bike hour</p><p><strong>Key strategy:</strong> The bike is the main fueling window (60–70 g/hr). Run: maximum gels + cola. Never go more than 45 min without eating on the bike.</p>",
    aa_nut_ironman: "<p><strong>CHO:</strong> 600–900 g total · 2:1 glucose:fructose required (up to 90g/hr)</p><p><strong>Fluids:</strong> 6–8 L total · 800ml/hr + heat adjustment</p><p><strong>Sodium:</strong> 7000–12000 mg · 700–1000mg/hr throughout the race</p><p><strong>Key strategy:</strong> Eat even when you're not hungry. In an Ironman the problem isn't eating too much, it's eating too little. Special bag at bike km 90 and run km 21.</p>",
